Deutsche Bank has invested in powerful GPU infrastructure and internally hosted open-weight AI models. dbHarness proposes to unlock the wider potential of these investments by building an internally owned, extensible AI agent platform that makes open-weight models practical and accessible for everyday engineering workflows.

We will begin by combining the open-source Pi agent harness with a capable coding model hosted in collaboration with dbLLM. The initial experiment will evaluate end-to-end software defect resolution, comparing accuracy, latency, developer effort, and cost against existing Gemini CLI and Copilot-assisted workflows. A hypothetical Claude Code subscription scenario will provide an additional commercial cost reference.

The objective is to validate whether an in-house coding agent can deliver competitive performance and favorable economics at scale, while developing the internal expertise to build, optimize, and maintain such solutions.

Our longer-term ambition is to collaborate with dbLLM and Ada to make open-weight AI a readily accessible option across Deutsche Bank. Beyond coding agents, this could enable broader adoption of internally hosted OCR, embedding, vision, and reasoning models across enterprise workflows, reducing avoidable reliance on external AI services, improving control over data processing, and maximizing the value of existing GPU investments.